.\" /usr/bin/tiffutil.
.TH TIFFUTIL 1 "September 25, 2026" "LibreDarwin"
.SH NAME
\fBtiffutil\fR \- convert and inspect TIFF image files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tiffutil
.I operation
.RI [ num ]
.I infile
.RB [ \-out
.IR outfile ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tiffutil
reads a TIFF file, prints information about it, or rewrites it, converting
the image data to a different compression scheme along the way.
.PP
Exactly one
.I operation
must be given, and it must be the first argument.  The
.I infile
is the argument that follows the operation, or follows the image number for
.BR \-extract .
An output file may be named with
.BR \-out ;
it must come after the input file.  Without
.BR \-out ,
the result is written to
.BR out.tiff .
.PP
The three
.BR \-info ,
.B \-verboseinfo
and
.B \-dump
operations take one or more input files and print to standard output.  They
do not accept
.BR \-out .
.PP
Rewritten files are always big-endian, carry a single strip with
.B RowsPerStrip
equal to the image height, and gain an ICC profile for their colour space that
the input may not have had.  Pixel values are preserved; only the container
around them changes.  When several images are present in a file, the
.BR \-none ,
.B \-lzw
and
.B \-packbits
operations rewrite the first one.
.SH OPERATIONS
.TP
.B \-none
Rewrite the file uncompressed.
.TP
.B \-lzw
Rewrite the file with LZW compression.  For 8-bit data the horizontal
differencing predictor is applied and recorded, which is what makes the
compression worthwhile.
.TP
.B \-packbits
Rewrite the file with PackBits compression.
.TP
.BI \-cat " infile1" " [infile2 ...]"
Concatenate the named files into one multi-directory TIFF.  The images must be
of the same size;
.B tiffutil
warns when they are not, because readers have no reliable way to choose
between them.
.TP
.BI \-catnosizecheck " infile1" " [infile2 ...]"
As
.BR \-cat ,
but the size check is suppressed.
.TP
.BI \-cathidpicheck " infile1" " [infile2 ...]"
As
.BR \-cat ,
but differing horizontal resolutions are tolerated.
.TP
.BI \-extract " num" " infile"
Write image
.I num
to a new file, keeping whatever compression that directory used.  Numbering
starts at 0.
.TP
.B \-info
Print a per-directory summary: geometry, depth, compression, resolution, and
whether an alpha channel or a profile is present.
.TP
.B \-verboseinfo
As
.BR \-info ,
and additionally print the offset and byte count of every strip.
.TP
.B \-dump
Print the raw image file directory: every tag, its type, count and value.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On success
.B tiffutil
prints
.RI \(lq N\  image s\  written\ to\  outfile .\(rq
to standard error and exits 0.  Nothing is written to standard output.
.PP
Failures print a message describing the problem, followed by a usage summary.
An output file is not created when an error is reported.
.PP
.B tiffutil
exits 1 for a usage error and 5 when an input file cannot be turned into an
image.
.SH EXAMPLES
Re-encode a file uncompressed:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tiffutil -none photo.tiff -out photo-raw.tiff
.fi
.RE
.PP
Compress it with LZW instead:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tiffutil -lzw photo.tiff -out photo-lzw.tiff
.fi
.RE
.PP
Build a multi-page file from two images of the same size:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tiffutil -cat page1.tiff page2.tiff -out pages.tiff
.fi
.RE
.PP
Pull one page back out of it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tiffutil -extract 1 pages.tiff -out page2.tiff
.fi
.RE
.PP
See what a file actually contains:
.PP
.RS
.nf
tiffutil -verboseinfo photo.tiff
.fi
.RE
.SH NOTES
.B \-cat
requires its inputs to be the same size, and says so on standard error when
they are not:
.PP
.RS
.nf
Warning: Sizes of concatenated images are not the same; this will lead
to problems in choosing the appropriate image in some cases.
.fi
.RE
.PP
The reference implementation of
.B tiffutil
crashes when
.B \-info
or
.B \-verboseinfo
is given a file that is missing or is not a TIFF.  This implementation reports
the error and exits non-zero instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tiff2icns (1),
.BR sips (1),
.BR iconutil (1),
.BR tops (1)
